<h1>How to Submit Your Claude Code Logs</h1>
<p>Full Stack Application Development &nbsp;·&nbsp; AI Workflow Submission Guide</p>
</header><!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════ --><!-- 1. WHAT ARE AI LOGS? --><!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="section">
<div class="section-header"><span class="sec-num">SECTION 1</span>
<h2>What Are AI Logs?</h2>
</div>
<div class="section-body">
<p>When you use <strong>Claude Code</strong>, every conversation you have with Claude is automatically saved to your computer as a <strong>.jsonl file</strong> (JSON Lines format). Each line in the file is a record of one message — your prompt, Claude's response, or a tool action Claude took on your behalf.</p>
<p>These log files are your evidence for <strong>C1 — AI Workflow</strong>. They show your tutor:</p>
<ol class="steps">
<li>
<div class="step-num">→</div>
<div class="step-body"><strong>What you asked Claude to do</strong> — your prompts across design, coding, testing, and deployment phases.</div>
</li>
<li>
<div class="step-num">→</div>
<div class="step-body"><strong>What Claude produced</strong> — the code, explanations, or suggestions it generated.</div>
</li>
<li>
<div class="step-num">→</div>
<div class="step-body"><strong>How your prompting evolved</strong> — whether you refined prompts, pushed back on Claude's output, or changed direction.</div>
</li>
</ol>
<div class="notice info"><span class="icon">🤖</span> <span>You do <strong>not</strong> need to manually write these logs — Claude Code creates them automatically. Your job is to copy them into your project repository before submitting.</span></div>
</div>
</section>
<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════ --><!-- 2. WHERE TO FIND YOUR LOGS --><!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="section">
<div class="section-header"><span class="sec-num">SECTION 2</span>
<h2>Where to Find Your Logs</h2>
</div>
<div class="section-body">
<p>Claude Code stores your conversation logs as <code>.jsonl</code> files in a hidden folder on your computer. The location depends on your operating system.</p>
<div class="code-block"><span class="comment"># Windows</span><br><span class="var">C:\Users\&lt;your-username&gt;\.claude\projects\&lt;code-folder&gt;\</span><br><br><span class="comment"># macOS / Linux</span><br><span class="var">~/.claude/projects/</span></div>
<p>Inside the <code>projects/</code> folder, each Claude Code project has its own subfolder. Inside that folder you will find <code>.jsonl</code> files — one file per conversation session.</p>
<div class="tree-wrap">
<div class="tree-label">Example log folder structure</div>
<div class="tree"><span class="folder">~/.claude/projects/</span> └── <span class="folder">your-project-id/</span> ├── <span class="hl">2024-11-01T09-32-11.jsonl</span> <span class="comment"># session on 1 Nov — design phase</span> ├── <span class="hl">2024-11-04T14-08-55.jsonl</span> <span class="comment"># session on 4 Nov — coding backend</span> ├── <span class="hl">2024-11-08T10-22-30.jsonl</span> <span class="comment"># session on 8 Nov — debugging</span> └── <span class="hl">2024-11-12T16-45-02.jsonl</span> <span class="comment"># session on 12 Nov — testing</span></div>
</div>
<div class="notice warn"><span class="icon">⚠️</span> <span>On Windows, folders starting with a dot (like <code>.claude</code>) may be hidden by default. To see it, open File Explorer, click <strong>View → Show → Hidden items</strong>, then navigate to <code>C:\Users\&lt;your-username&gt;\.claude\projects\</code>.</span></div>
</div>
</section>
<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════ --><!-- 3. BACKUP YOUR LOGS --><!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="section">
<div class="section-header"><span class="sec-num">SECTION 3</span>
<h2>Backup Your Logs</h2>
</div>
<div class="section-body">
<p>Before you commit your code, copy any new <code>.jsonl</code> files from your Claude project log folder into your repository under <code>ai/&lt;your-name&gt;/ai-logs/</code>. This is your submission folder for C1.</p>
<h3>Where to copy them</h3>
<div class="tree-wrap">
<div class="tree-label">Your repository</div>
<div class="tree"><span class="folder">your-project/</span> └── <span class="folder">ai/</span> └── <span class="folder">&lt;your-name&gt;/</span> └── <span class="folder">ai-logs/</span> ├── <span class="hl">2024-11-01T09-32-11.jsonl</span> ├── <span class="hl">2024-11-04T14-08-55.jsonl</span> ├── <span class="hl">2024-11-08T10-22-30.jsonl</span> └── <span class="hl">2024-11-12T16-45-02.jsonl</span></div>
</div>
<h3>Step-by-step</h3>
<ol class="steps">
<li>
<div class="step-num">1</div>
<div class="step-body"><span class="step-title">Open your Claude log folder</span>
<p>Navigate to <code>~/.claude/projects/&lt;your-project-id&gt;/</code> on macOS, or <code>C:\Users\&lt;your-username&gt;\.claude\projects\&lt;code-folder&gt;\</code> on Windows.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<div class="step-num">2</div>
<div class="step-body"><span class="step-title">Copy any new .jsonl files</span>
<p>Identify any <code>.jsonl</code> files you haven't copied yet. Copy them — don't move them. The originals stay in the Claude folder.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<div class="step-num">3</div>
<div class="step-body"><span class="step-title">Paste into your repository</span>
<p>Paste the files into <code>ai/&lt;your-name&gt;/ai-logs/</code> inside your project repository.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<div class="step-num">4</div>
<div class="step-body"><span class="step-title">Commit and push</span>
<p>Include the log files in your next commit. Use a clear message like <code>add ai logs for week 3</code>.</p>
</div>
</li>
</ol>
<div class="notice tip"><span class="icon">✅</span> <span>Don't wait until the final submission to copy your logs. <strong>Back them up regularly</strong> — after each working session is ideal. This also shows your tutor that you used AI throughout the project, not just at the end.</span></div>
</div>
</section>
<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════ --><!-- 4. STARTING A NEW CHAT WITHOUT LOSING PROGRESS --><!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="section">
<div class="section-header"><span class="sec-num">SECTION 4</span>
<h2>Starting a New Chat Without Losing Progress</h2>
</div>
<div class="section-body">
<p>Claude Code conversations have a <strong>context limit</strong>. When a chat gets very long — or when you start working on a different part of the project — Claude may lose track of earlier decisions. Starting a new chat solves this, but you don't want to lose the context of where you left off.</p>
<p>The solution is a <strong>handoff summary</strong>: ask Claude to write a summary of the current session before you close it, then paste that summary into your next chat to instantly pick up where you left off.</p>
<hr class="divider">
<h3>Step 1 — Generate a handoff summary</h3>
<p>At the end of your current session, type the following prompt into Claude Code:</p>
<div class="prompt-block"><span class="prompt-label">Prompt to Claude — end of session</span> <span class="hl">Please write a handoff summary for this session.</span> Include:<br>- What we were working on and the current state<br>- Key decisions made and the reasoning behind them<br>- Files created or modified<br>- What still needs to be done<br>- Any known issues or things to watch out for</div>
<p>Claude will produce a structured summary. <strong>Copy it and save it</strong> — paste it into a note, a text file, or directly into your next chat.</p>
<div class="handoff-box">
<div class="handoff-box-header">Example handoff summary (Claude's output)</div>
<div class="handoff-box-body">
<p><strong>Current state:</strong> We built the user authentication flow — registration and login endpoints are complete and tested. JWT tokens are issued on login and verified via middleware.</p>
<p><strong>Key decisions:</strong> Used <code>bcrypt</code> with 10 salt rounds for password hashing. Chose short-lived access tokens (15 min) with refresh token logic to be added next.</p>
<p><strong>Files modified:</strong> <code>backend/src/routes/auth.js</code>, <code>backend/src/middleware/auth.js</code>, <code>backend/src/models/User.js</code></p>
<p><strong>Still to do:</strong> Refresh token endpoint, protected route testing, frontend login form integration.</p>
<p><strong>Watch out for:</strong> The <code>JWT_SECRET</code> env variable must be set in Render before deploying — deployment will fail without it.</p>
</div>
</div>
<hr class="divider">
<h3>Step 2 — Start a new chat with the handoff summary</h3>
<p>Open a new Claude Code chat and begin with the following prompt, pasting your summary where indicated:</p>
<div class="prompt-block"><span class="prompt-label">Prompt to Claude — start of new session</span> <span class="hl">I'm continuing work on my full stack project.</span> Here is a summary of where we left off:<br><br><span class="str">[paste your handoff summary here]</span><br><br>Let's continue from here. Today I want to work on <span class="str">[describe your goal for this session]</span>.</div>
<p>Claude will read the summary and have the full context it needs to continue helping you effectively — without you needing to re-explain everything.</p>
<div class="notice tip"><span class="icon">✅</span> <span>A good handoff summary is also useful for <strong>your own reflection</strong>. Keep a copy in a <code>notes/</code> folder or your personal notes app — it helps you write your <code>ai-reflection.md</code> at the end of the project.</span></div>
<div class="notice warn"><span class="icon">⚠️</span> <span>Remember to <strong>back up your logs</strong> before starting a new chat. Each chat session creates a new <code>.jsonl</code> file — copy the completed session's file into <code>ai/&lt;your-name&gt;/ai-logs/</code> before you close it.</span></div>
</div>
</section>
</div></body></html>
